We investigate general differential equations that describe electrostatic behavior in microelectromechanical systems, represented by the equation:

\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
\varphi(r,u'(r)) = \lambda\int_0^r\frac{f(s)}{g(u(s))}ds,  & r\in(0,1), \\
0 < u(r) < 1,  & r\in(0,1), \\
u(1) = 0, 
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

where $\varphi$, $g$, and $f$ are specific functions on the interval $[0,1]$ and $\lambda > 0$ is a parameter. This study focuses on proving the existence and regularity of a solution to the problem \eqref{P}, while also determining upper and lower bounds for the pull-in voltage. Furthermore, when $\varphi(r,v) = r^\alpha |v|^\beta v$, we derive the exact asymptotic behavior of the solution near the origin.
